      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">You Are Not Imagining It</h2>
      <p>If you have ever sat in a meeting that felt perfectly ordinary to everyone else and felt your heart hammering, your palms sweating, and your mind convinced that something was catastrophically wrong, you know what it is like when anxiety arrives uninvited. You may have told yourself to calm down. You may have felt embarrassed. You may have wondered what was wrong with you.</p>
      <p>Nothing is wrong with you. What you experienced was your nervous system working exactly as it evolved to work, just applying that response to the wrong situation.</p>
      <p>According to data reviewed by the Public Health Agency of Canada, one in four Canadians will meet the criteria for an anxiety disorder at some point in their lifetime.  Yet despite how incredibly common anxiety is, it remains one of the most misunderstood experiences people carry. Many live with it for years before seeking support, often because they believe they simply need to try harder, think more positively, or toughen up. This article is here to tell you that none of those approaches address what is actually happening in your body and brain.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">What Is Actually Happening When You Feel Anxious</h2>
      <p>At its core, anxiety is a survival mechanism. Deep inside your brain sits a small, almond-shaped region called the amygdala. Its job is to act as your personal alarm system, constantly scanning your environment for anything that might be dangerous. It does this faster than conscious thought, faster than reason, faster than logic.</p>
      <p>When your amygdala detects something it perceives as a threat, it sends a distress signal to the hypothalamus, which then tells your autonomic nervous system to begin preparing your body to respond. Your sympathetic nervous system quickly readies your body to confront or escape the threat. This happens in the blink of an eye.</p> 
      <p>The result is what most people know as the fight-or-flight response. Your heart rate climbs to pump blood to your muscles. Your breathing becomes shallow and rapid to take in more oxygen. Your digestion slows because it is not a priority when you are facing danger. Your muscles tense. Your vision sharpens. Your body floods with adrenaline and cortisol, the primary stress hormones.</p>
      <p>This response is brilliant when you are in genuine physical danger. It has kept humans alive for hundreds of thousands of years. The problem is that the amygdala is not particularly good at distinguishing between a predator in the wild and a difficult email from your manager, a crowded subway car, or a social situation that makes you feel out of place. To the amygdala, a threat is a threat.</p>
      <p>Anxiety can be understood as anticipatory stress, a heightened vigilance for something that could happen but has not happened yet. When this response stays switched on without an immediate danger to respond to, your body remains in a state of constant high alert, with symptoms that can include a racing heart, shallow breathing, difficulty thinking clearly, excessive sweating, and a feeling of being shaky or out of control.</p> 

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">Why Some People Experience More Anxiety Than Others</h2>
      <p>Anxiety does not affect everyone equally, and this is important to understand. Your level of anxiety is not a measure of your strength or your resilience. It is shaped by a complex combination of factors that includes your biology, your nervous system's sensitivity, your early life experiences, and the environment you are living in right now.</p>
      <p>People who grew up in unpredictable or stressful environments often have nervous systems that learned to stay on high alert because it was genuinely safer to do so. The body adapted. That adaptation may have been a gift at the time. In adult life, in a world that is mostly safe, that same nervous system can create significant difficulty.</p>
      <p>Trauma plays a substantial role too. Unprocessed traumatic experiences can keep the amygdala in a state of heightened sensitivity, meaning it fires more easily and more intensely in response to stimuli that others might barely notice. This is not a malfunction. It is the nervous system remembering and trying to protect you.</p>
      <p>Research published in December 2024 by Statistics Canada found that Canadians in lower-income households had significantly higher odds of experiencing anxiety disorders compared with those in the highest income households, highlighting how social and economic stressors play a direct role in anxiety prevalence.</p> 
      <p>Genetics also contribute. If anxiety runs in your family, your nervous system may be naturally more reactive. This does not mean you are destined to suffer. It means you may need to be more intentional about how you support your nervous system, and that support is genuinely available.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">The Different Forms Anxiety Takes</h2>
      <p>Anxiety is not one single experience. It shows up in many forms, and understanding which type resonates most with your experience is an important first step toward getting the right support.</p>
      <p>Generalized Anxiety Disorder, or GAD, involves persistent, wide-ranging worry about many different areas of life, work, health, relationships, finances, and the future. An estimated 2.4 million Canadians will report symptoms consistent with a GAD diagnosis in their lifetime, making it the most common anxiety disorder seen in primary care settings.  The worry in GAD is difficult to control, feels disproportionate to the actual situation, and comes with physical symptoms like tension, fatigue, and sleep difficulties.</p>
      <p>Social anxiety is the intense fear of being judged, embarrassed, or humiliated in social or performance situations. It is often misread as shyness or introversion, but it goes much deeper. The anticipatory dread before a social event, the exhausting mental replaying of conversations afterward, and the avoidance that gradually narrows a person's life are hallmarks of social anxiety that therapy can genuinely address.</p>
      <p>Panic disorder involves recurring, unexpected panic attacks, which are sudden surges of intense fear accompanied by physical symptoms so severe they are often mistaken for a heart attack. The fear of having another panic attack can become its own source of anxiety, creating a cycle that feels impossible to break without support.</p>
      <p>Health anxiety, phobias, and obsessive-compulsive patterns are also anxiety-related experiences that many people live with silently for years, often not realizing that effective treatment exists.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">Why You Cannot Simply Think Your Way Out of Anxiety</h2>
      <p>One of the most frustrating things about anxiety is how unhelpful positive thinking is when you are in the middle of it. This is not because you are not trying hard enough. It is because anxiety lives in the body and in the older, faster parts of the brain, not primarily in the rational, thinking mind.</p>
      <p>Because nervous system responses happen automatically, trying to simply think your way out of them often does not work well. Change tends to happen through awareness, safety, and experience. Learning to recognize your nervous system patterns with curiosity rather than self-criticism often makes it easier to work with them over time.</p> 
      <p>This is also why willpower alone rarely helps with anxiety in the long run. You cannot reason your amygdala into standing down. You can, however, work with your nervous system through the right approaches, and that is exactly what therapy is designed to help you do.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://cmha.ca/brochure/anxiety-disorders/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);">Canadian Mental Health Association</a> notes that anxiety disorders are among the most treatable of all mental health conditions when addressed with the right support. The key word is addressed. Avoiding anxiety, pushing it down, or simply enduring it rarely leads to lasting relief.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">What Therapy for Anxiety Actually Looks Like</h2>
      <p>Many people come to therapy imagining that they will be told to simply breathe deeply and think more positively. That is not what good therapy for anxiety involves.</p>
      <p>Cognitive Behavioural Therapy, or CBT, is one of the most thoroughly researched approaches to anxiety. It works by helping you identify the thought patterns that fuel your anxiety, examine whether those patterns are accurate, and gradually build a different relationship with anxious thoughts and the situations that trigger them. At Twish, CBT is used as one tool among many, shaped specifically around your experience.</p>
      <p>Mindfulness-based approaches help you build the capacity to notice anxious thoughts and physical sensations without immediately reacting to them. Over time, this creates more space between trigger and response, giving you genuine choices about how to act even when anxiety is present.</p>
      <p>Where anxiety is connected to past experiences or trauma, which it very often is, trauma-informed care creates a safe container to begin exploring those connections at a pace that feels manageable. Healing happens when the nervous system feels safe enough to begin processing what it has been holding.</p>
      <p>Data from Statistics Canada shows that more than one third of Canadians who met the criteria for a mood or anxiety disorder in 2022 reported unmet or partially met mental health care needs.  There are real barriers to accessing support in this country. At Twish, Shimul works with a sliding scale for clients facing financial barriers, because the belief that therapy should be accessible is not just a statement. It is a commitment.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">Practical Things That Help Right Now</h2>
      <p>While working with a therapist is the most effective long-term approach to anxiety, there are things you can do right now that genuinely help regulate your nervous system in the moment.</p>
      <p>Slow, extended exhales activate the parasympathetic nervous system, which is the part of your nervous system responsible for rest and recovery. Breathing in through your nose and exhaling slowly helps engage the part of your nervous system that acts as a brake on the fight-or-flight response.  Try breathing in for four counts and out for six. Do this for two to three minutes. It will not eliminate anxiety entirely, but it will begin to signal to your nervous system that you are safe.</p>
      <p>Physical movement helps metabolize the adrenaline and cortisol that anxiety floods your system with. Even a ten-minute walk can genuinely reduce the intensity of anxious feelings in the moment.</p>
      <p>Grounding practices, things that orient you to the present through your senses, interrupt the anxious mind's tendency to project into worst-case futures. Noticing five things you can see, four you can touch, three you can hear, two you can smell, and one you can taste is not a gimmick. It works because it redirects the brain's attention to the present, where the danger the amygdala is responding to usually does not actually exist.</p>
      <p>Reducing caffeine, prioritizing sleep, and limiting time on social media are not glamorous suggestions, but the evidence for their impact on anxiety levels is consistent and clear.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.85rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;">When to Seek Support</h2>
      <p>If anxiety is interrupting your sleep, your relationships, your work, or your enjoyment of daily life, that is the signal. You do not need to be in crisis to deserve support. You do not need to have hit a breaking point. The threshold for reaching out is simply this: if anxiety is making your life smaller, it is time to talk to someone.</p>
      <p>Many people wait years, sometimes decades, before seeking help for anxiety. Some never do. And that is a real loss, because with the right support, anxiety does not have to define your experience. The nervous system is genuinely capable of learning to respond differently. That capacity for change, for healing, is not wishful thinking. It is biology.</p>
      <p>If you are ready to understand your anxiety more deeply and begin working through it, Shimul offers a <a href="https://twishcare.myndweb.app/shimul/booking" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);">free 15-minute consultation</a> with no commitment and no pressure. It is simply a conversation to see if working together feels like the right fit.</p>

